# Copy to hooks/state-check.conf to turn on two paired guards for a multi-session or
# multi-role project — hooks/stop-state-check.sh (a Stop hook) and hooks/doorman.sh (a
# UserPromptSubmit hook). Both read this ONE file. No file at all: both guards do nothing
# for every session — the safe default for a single-session project with no roles to track.
#
# One optional line, anywhere in the file:
#   STATE_FILE <path>
# {role} in <path> is replaced with the matched role name below. Default if this line is
# left out: docs/state/{role}.md
#
# Every other non-blank, non-comment line:
#   <folder-glob>  <role-name>  <floor-in-thousands-of-tokens>
# <folder-glob> is matched against the BASENAME of the session's working directory (a git
# worktree name, e.g. "myapp-planner-3", or a shared root folder's own name) with a plain
# bash `case` glob — "myapp-planner*" matches "myapp-planner-3"; "myapp-*" matches any
# other worktree. The FIRST matching line wins, so put more specific patterns first.
# <role-name> is free text — it fills {role} in STATE_FILE and labels the inbox/retired
# files. <floor-in-thousands-of-tokens> is when stop-state-check.sh marks that session
# "retired": its context at or over this many thousand tokens. Pick a number well under
# your model's real context window, so a session still has room to write its state file and
# hand off cleanly before it runs out.
#
# A folder whose name matches no line here is untouched by either guard. The name matched is the
# git TOPLEVEL folder's (a chat opened in a subfolder of a seat's worktree matches the same line),
# both as written and after lower-casing and cutting at the first digit ("myapp-dev-3" also reads
# "myapp-dev", so "myapp-dev*" and "myapp-dev" both match it).
#
# <role-name> may be the word auto: the role is then read from the folder name itself — lower-cased,
# cut at the first digit, trailing hyphen dropped ("myapp-planner-3" -> "myapp-planner") — and any
# ROLE_ALIAS line below renames it. Example row:   myapp-*  auto  800
#
# More optional key lines (each one line, anywhere in the file; all defaults below are
# illustrative — pick numbers for your own model and habits):
#   ROLE_ALIAS <folder-role> <role>   read the folder-derived role "<folder-role>" as "<role>"
#                                      e.g.  ROLE_ALIAS myapp-planner planner
#   WRAPUP_K <n>          at the first turn end past <n> thousand tokens, ask the chat to finish,
#                          land, update its state file and say it is ready to retire. Default 600.
#   WRAPUP_ROLES <r> ...  which roles get that wrap-up ("*" = every role). Default: the dev seats
#                          of MULTI-CHAT-ROLES.md: dev senior junior designer senior-dev junior-dev
#   TELL_GROWTH_K <n>     a turn that grew the context by <n> thousand or more with no helper
#                          (Agent/Workflow) launched leaves a one-line nudge for the next prompt. Default 30.
#   TELL_EXEMPT_ROLES <r> ...   roles that never get that nudge. Default none.
#   UNATTENDED_UNTIL <path>     the unattended-run file: ONE line, an end time in epoch seconds.
#                          While that time is in the future the wrap-up is skipped (an overnight run
#                          nobody is watching). A missing, garbled or past file is a normal run.
#                          Default ~/.claude/state/unattended-until (a leading ~/ is expanded).
#                          Nothing in this kit writes the file. To set it by hand for 8 hours:
#                            echo $(( $(date +%s) + 8*3600 )) > ~/.claude/state/unattended-until
#                          and to end the run early:  rm ~/.claude/state/unattended-until
STATE_FILE docs/state/{role}.md
myapp-planner*   planner   500
myapp-*          dev       850
myapp            root      850
# ROLE_ALIAS myapp-planner planner
# WRAPUP_K 600
# WRAPUP_ROLES dev senior junior designer senior-dev junior-dev
# TELL_GROWTH_K 30
# TELL_EXEMPT_ROLES planner advisor
# UNATTENDED_UNTIL ~/.claude/state/unattended-until
